      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Neural style transfer with the GENERIC cv2.dnn nodes (mosaic-9.onnx)\n\nAfter the pyimagesearch neural-style-transfer tutorial, but built entirely from the reusable cv2.dnn primitives - no model-specific node. Same three generic nodes as workflow 40, just a different model and a different clip step:\n\n  Load Image\n   -> DNN Blob From Image   (cv2.dnn.blobFromImage: scale 1.0, swap R/B, native size, NO mean)\n   -> DNN Forward           (loads mosaic-9.onnx, runs one forward pass)\n   -> DNN Images From Blob  (cv2.dnn.imagesFromBlob: undoes the CHW->HWC transpose)\n   -> CV Cast Array (uint8)  (the CLIP: saturating round to 0-255, like numpy.clip)\n   -> CV Array -> Image     (RGB: back to a ComfyUI IMAGE)\n\nPreprocessing differs from the NAFNet deblur of workflow 40: this ONNX fast-neural-style model wants a RAW [0-255] RGB blob (scale 1.0, no mean subtraction) - NOT the ImageNet mean subtraction the tutorial uses for its Torch .t7 models, and NOT the 1/255 scale NAFNet needs.\n\nThe postprocess CLIP is 'CV Cast Array' to uint8, NOT cv2.convertScaleAbs. The style output runs well outside [0,255] on BOTH sides (roughly -260..380), and convertScaleAbs takes the ABSOLUTE value - it would reflect the negative (dark) pixels back to bright and corrupt them. 'CV Cast Array' rounds and clamps to 0-255 with no abs (a true numpy.clip), which is exactly the tutorial's postprocess.\n\nNote: the network rounds each output side up to a multiple of 4, so the styled image can be a pixel or two larger than the input (before/after are previewed separately rather than slider-compared for that reason). Inference is heavy (seconds for a ~1 MP image on CPU)."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
